# Spec Document: Subproject 9 - SEO, Meta, & Performance

## Overview
This subproject configures Next.js native SEO and metadata, implements a sitemap and `robots.txt`, establishes progressive web app (PWA) assets like `manifest.json`, and ensures strict image optimization across the application to achieve a 100/100 Lighthouse performance score.

## Tech Stack & Libraries
- **Framework:** Next.js (App Router Metadata API)
- **Image Optimization:** `next/image`
- **SEO Utilities:** Next.js dynamic route handlers (`sitemap.ts`, `robots.ts`)

## Implementation Details

### 1. Global & Per-Page Metadata (`app/layout.tsx` & Page Level)
- Utilize the Next.js `Metadata` API in `layout.tsx` to set the global fallback metadata.
  - **Site Name:** Ehtesham Zeya | Portfolio
  - **Description:** Data enthusiast and engineering student specializing in Materials Science, AI, and predictive modeling.
  - **OpenGraph/Twitter:** Configure `og:image` and `twitter:card` with a placeholder URL pointing to `public/og-image.png`.
- Add specific `metadata` exports to individual pages:
  - `app/projects/page.tsx`: Update title to "Projects | Ehtesham Zeya".
  - `app/resume/page.tsx`: Update title to "Resume | Ehtesham Zeya".
  - `app/blog/page.tsx`: Update title to "Terminal Blog | Ehtesham Zeya".

### 2. Search Engine Directives (`app/sitemap.ts`, `app/robots.ts`)
- **Sitemap:** Create `app/sitemap.ts` to programmatically generate a `sitemap.xml` that includes the root `/`, `/projects`, `/blog`, `/resume`, and dynamically maps over `content/blog.ts` and `content/projects.ts` for individual slugs.
- **Robots.txt:** Create `app/robots.ts` to allow all web crawlers (`User-Agent: *`, `Allow: /`) and provide the absolute URL to the sitemap.

### 3. App Icons & Manifest (`public/`)
- Create a `manifest.json` in the `public/` directory establishing the terminal-themed app name, short name, and theme colors (e.g., `#0f172a` for standard dark slate).
- Ensure a placeholder `favicon.ico` and `apple-icon.png` are referenced properly in the root layout.

### 4. Performance & Image Optimization
- Audit all components (Hero, About, Project Cards). If any standard HTML `<img>` tags were generated in previous steps, refactor them to use Next.js `<Image />` components.
- Ensure all images have explicit `width`, `height`, and `alt` attributes to prevent Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).

## Rules for Implementation
- **Regression Prevention (CRITICAL):** During metadata and code optimization, you must NOT alter the established page structures. 
  1. Do NOT add GitHub links or icons next to any project entries.
  2. The section titled "Leadership/Extracurricular" must remain in the final section of the page layout, immediately before the footer.
- **Theme Color:** The metadata `theme-color` or `color-scheme` must be explicitly set to `dark` to prevent browser flashes of white before the CSS loads.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Next.js app compiles and runs with zero errors (`npm run dev`).
- [ ] `<head>` contains valid title, description, OpenGraph, and Twitter metadata tags upon inspection.
- [ ] `localhost:3000/sitemap.xml` and `localhost:3000/robots.txt` render valid data.
- [ ] No raw `<img>` tags exist in the codebase (excluding markdown content parsed dynamically).
- [ ] Lighthouse audit (via Chrome DevTools) scores 90+ in Performance, Accessibility, Best Practices, and SEO.
